---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'signals', label: 'Signals' },
   { id: 'states', label: 'The five states' },
   { id: 'commands', label: 'Running inspect' },
   { id: 'limits', label: 'What it does not do' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Applicability"
   summary="Applicability answers one question: does this page contain anything a given success criterion is about? a11ied reads the HTML for 15 kinds of signal, matches them against criterion tags, and returns a state for every criterion in the version."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="signals">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Signals</p>
            <h2>What the analyzer looks for</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>inspect</code> fetches the URL and parses the HTML. It does not start a browser.
            Each signal carries a category, the source it came from, the text that matched, and a
            confidence of high, medium, or low.
         </p>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"signals": [
  &#123; "category": "heading", "source": "dom", "value": "heading structure", "confidence": "high" &#125;,
  &#123; "category": "form", "source": "dom", "value": "form controls", "confidence": "high" &#125;
]</code></pre>
         <p>
            The 15 categories are <code>auth</code>, <code>dialog</code>,
            <code>drag-and-drop</code>, <code>form</code>, <code>heading</code>, <code>help</code>,
            <code>landmark</code>, <code>live-region</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>menu</code>,
            <code>overlay</code>, <code>repeated-form</code>, <code>tablist</code>,
            <code>validation</code>, and <code>widget</code>.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Sources</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            A signal comes from the DOM, the accessibility tree, document metadata, a W3C Quickref
            tag, or a hint you supplied.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="states">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">States</p>
         <h2>The five applicability states</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>applicable</code>
            <span>A high-confidence signal matches the criterion's tags.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>likely-applicable</code>
            <span>A signal matches, but not strongly enough to call it applicable.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>not-detected</code>
            <span>Nothing on the page matched. The criterion may still apply.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>out-of-scope</code>
            <span>The criterion does not apply to this kind of target.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>unknown</code>
            <span>The signals do not support a decision either way.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="commands">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Commands</p>
         <h2>Running inspect</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>inspect applicable</code> returns a matrix with one assessment per criterion.
         <code>inspect criterion</code> narrows it to one.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 inspect applicable --url http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 inspect criterion 2.4.3 --url http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"assessment": &#123;
  "criterionId": "2.4.3",
  "state": "likely-applicable",
  "reasons": [
    "Detected heading and form signals (heading structure and form controls) and matching criterion tags (structure, forms, controls, and errors)."
  ],
  "matchedSignalCategories": ["heading", "form"],
  "matchedTags": ["structure", "forms", "controls", "errors"]
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>reasons</code> names the signals and the tags that produced the state. Read it before
         acting on the state.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="limits">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Limits</p>
         <h2>What applicability does not tell you</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         An assessment never says pass or fail. It says which criteria are worth testing on this
         page, so a sweep of 86 criteria becomes a short list.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>not-detected</code> is the state to be careful with. The analyzer reads the HTML the
         server returned. Content a client framework renders later, or a dialog that only exists
         after a click, produces no signal. Use <a href="/coverage">coverage</a> to decide what
         evidence a criterion needs, and a driver session to reach states the initial HTML does not
         contain.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
